            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"The bill of divorcement primarily symbolizes God's covenant lawsuit, judgment, and judicial separation (exile) from unfaithful Israel due to spiritual adultery. It also refers to the literal Mosaic legal provision regulating marriage dissolution.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"The bill of divorcement represents the execution of God's covenant lawsuit, resulting in exile and scattering. It notes that while God hates the betrayal of a true covenant wife, divorce is commanded for false unions (fornication), and that Christ's death at the cross legally answered the 'no-return' rule for divorced Israel.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"God's covenant lawsuit and judicial separation/exile from unfaithful Israel.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The executed covenant lawsuit resulting in exile and scattering, along with its specific legal and typological resolution.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"EQUIVALENT\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The book adopts the primary consensus meaning but explicitly incorporates a specific minority typological view (that Christ's death resolved the Deuteronomy 24 'no-return' rule to remarry Israel) and includes the Ezra 10 application where divorce is commanded for illicit unions.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"None. The book aligns with the primary consensus and elevates a recognized minority typological extension to sit alongside it.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The core referent of both the book and the consensus is the same: the covenant lawsuit and subsequent exile of Israel. The book refines the concept by systematically integrating the Deuteronomy 24 'no-return' rule resolved at the cross and the Ezra 10 exception for false marriages, broadening the theological application.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"The integration of the 'no-return rule at the cross' may be viewed as a highly specific, systemic theological construct (Two-House or specific typological framework) rather than the direct, primary symbolic meaning of the prophetic divorce motif itself.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
